/// =====================================================================================
/// Relationships
///
/// Every relationship here corresponds to a `NOT ENFORCED` FOREIGN KEY declared in
/// `src/warehouse/ddl/04_constraints.sql`, and that correspondence is deliberate rather than
/// incidental: relationship auto-detection over the SQL analytics endpoint reads those declared
/// FKs, so the constraints file is what makes this file mostly automatic instead of
/// hand-drawn. `tests/test_semantic_model.py` asserts the two sets match in both directions —
/// a relationship here with no FK behind it, or an FK with no relationship, both fail.
///
/// Three properties are set on every single one, and the defaults are wrong for all three:
///
///   `crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection` — the default for a many-to-one is already single,
///   but stating it stops the "just make it bidirectional to fix my visual" edit that
///   introduces ambiguous filter paths a year later. With two facts sharing five dimensions,
///   one bidirectional relationship is enough to make a measure's value depend on which
///   visual it is in.
///
///   `isKey` is *not* used on the many side, obviously — but note the one side is the
///   **surrogate** key for every dimension, never the business key. For the three SCD2
///   dimensions this is the whole point: `dim_account` holds several rows per `account_id`,
///   so a relationship on `account_id` would be many-to-many and would re-resolve the version
///   at query time — restating history every time a risk band changes. Joining on
///   `account_sk` freezes the version the loader chose by comparing `auth_ts` to the validity
///   interval. Point-in-time correctness is a *modelling* property here, not just an ETL one.
///
///   Referential integrity is **not** declared (`relyOnReferentialIntegrity` is absent).
///   Direct Lake does not use it the way DirectQuery does, and asserting it would be asserting
///   something no engine enforces — `04_constraints.sql` is explicit that the DQ layer is the
///   actual guarantee. Every FK resolves because the loaders COALESCE to the -1 unknown
///   member, so there is nothing to gain by claiming it and a wrong-results failure mode if
///   the claim ever stopped holding.
/// =====================================================================================

/// fact_transaction → dim_date on the authorisation date. One date role on this fact, so this
/// one is unambiguous and active.
relationship rel_ft_date
	fromColumn: fact_transaction.date_sk
	toColumn: dim_date.date_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_ft_account
	fromColumn: fact_transaction.account_sk
	toColumn: dim_account.account_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

/// Present *and* redundant-looking, because a customer can be reached through dim_account too.
/// It is kept because the path through dim_account is a path through a *versioned* dimension:
/// filtering customers via account would filter to the account versions in context, not to the
/// customer. Two direct relationships, one ambiguous path avoided — which is why
/// dim_account → dim_customer is deliberately absent from this file.
relationship rel_ft_customer
	fromColumn: fact_transaction.customer_sk
	toColumn: dim_customer.customer_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_ft_merchant
	fromColumn: fact_transaction.merchant_sk
	toColumn: dim_merchant.merchant_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_ft_card_product
	fromColumn: fact_transaction.card_product_sk
	toColumn: dim_card_product.card_product_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_ft_currency
	fromColumn: fact_transaction.currency_sk
	toColumn: dim_currency.currency_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

/// Resolves to the -1 unknown member for every approved transaction, which is a legitimate
/// "not applicable" rather than a failed lookup (03_facts.sql says so at the column). The
/// consequence for this model: a decline-reason slicer with no explicit filter includes
/// approvals under whatever `dim_decline_reason` calls row -1, so
/// `[Decline Rate by Reason]` in measures.dax is written to be safe under that slicer rather
/// than assuming it is never applied.
relationship rel_ft_decline_reason
	fromColumn: fact_transaction.decline_reason_sk
	toColumn: dim_decline_reason.decline_reason_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// fact_dispute — the role-playing dimension, and the only interesting part of this file
/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// `fact_dispute` has two FKs to `dim_date`: `raised_date_sk` and `resolved_date_sk`
/// (04_constraints.sql:189-195). A star cannot have two active relationships between the same
/// pair of tables — the filter path would be ambiguous and the engine rejects it — so one is
/// active and one is not, and the inactive one is switched on per measure with
/// `USERELATIONSHIP`.
///
/// **Raised is the active role, and that is a business decision, not a coin toss.** The
/// default meaning of "disputes in March" is disputes *raised* in March: it is the number that
/// is final the moment it happens, and the one an operations team is measured on. Resolved-date
/// counts restate — a dispute raised in March and resolved in May moves out of March's
/// resolved figure into May's, so a report built on resolved-by-default shows last month's
/// numbers changing every week. Whichever role is active becomes the answer to every
/// un-thought-about question, so it should be the one that does not move.
///
/// The measures that genuinely need the other role say so explicitly:
/// `[Dispute Resolution Days]` and `[Disputes Resolved]` in measures.dax both wrap
/// `USERELATIONSHIP(fact_dispute[resolved_date_sk], dim_date[date_sk])`.
///
/// The alternative — a second date dimension (`dim_resolved_date`) as a view alias — is the
/// usual pattern and is *worse here*. Two date tables means two date hierarchies in the field
/// list, only one can be the model's marked date table for time intelligence, and on Fabric an
/// alias view over a warehouse table is a maintenance item that has to be kept in step with
/// `dim_date` by hand. `04_constraints.sql:178-181` commits to this choice in writing; this is
/// where that commitment is honoured.
relationship rel_fd_raised_date
	fromColumn: fact_dispute.raised_date_sk
	toColumn: dim_date.date_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

/// Inactive, and switched on by USERELATIONSHIP where the resolved role is what is meant.
///
/// `resolved_date_sk` is NOT NULL on an open dispute too — it resolves to the -1 unknown
/// member rather than being left NULL (03_facts.sql). So a measure using this role must
/// exclude open disputes itself; the relationship will happily count them all against
/// whatever date row -1 is. That is the trap in this pattern and the reason
/// `[Disputes Resolved]` filters on `is_open = 0` rather than trusting the join.
relationship rel_fd_resolved_date
	isActive: false
	fromColumn: fact_dispute.resolved_date_sk
	toColumn: dim_date.date_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_fd_account
	fromColumn: fact_dispute.account_sk
	toColumn: dim_account.account_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_fd_merchant
	fromColumn: fact_dispute.merchant_sk
	toColumn: dim_merchant.merchant_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_fd_currency
	fromColumn: fact_dispute.currency_sk
	toColumn: dim_currency.currency_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// agg_merchant_daily
/// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Two relationships only, because the table has two keys: it is a merchant-day grain and
/// nothing else. It is exposed as its own table rather than registered as a user-defined
/// **aggregation** over `fact_transaction`, and the reason is in README.md under
/// "Why agg_merchant_daily is not an aggregation".
relationship rel_agg_date
	fromColumn: agg_merchant_daily.date_sk
	toColumn: dim_date.date_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection

relationship rel_agg_merchant
	fromColumn: agg_merchant_daily.merchant_sk
	toColumn: dim_merchant.merchant_sk
	crossFilteringBehavior: oneDirection
